\title{Introduction to MTRClust}
\author{Hasan Bulut}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Multivariate Panel Data}

MTRClust expects a numeric array with dimensions \(n \times T \times p\):
units, ordered time points, and variables. The fitting routine requires complete
finite numeric data; missing values are not imputed.

\section{Small Reproducible Example}

<<setup, echo=TRUE, results=hide>>=
library(MTRClust)
dat <- simulate_mtr_panel(n = 30, tt = 15, p = 3, seed = 11)
dim(dat$x)
@

The main user function is \texttt{mtrclust()}. The example below keeps the
iteration budget deliberately small so that package checks remain fast.

<<fit, echo=TRUE>>=
fit <- mtrclust(dat$x, k = 3, alpha = 0.05, rank = 2,
                nstart = 2, max_iter = 20, seed = 11)
fit
@

\section{Interpreting Results}

The returned object has class \texttt{mtrclust}. It records cluster assignments,
retained and trimmed units, fitted trajectories, objective values, termination
status, rollback status, and multilevel outlier diagnostics.

<<summary, echo=TRUE>>=
summary(fit)
mtrclust_diagnostics(fit)
head(mtrclust_diagnostics(fit, type = "units"))
@

\section{Termination and Reproducibility}

\texttt{fit\$termination} records why the algorithm stopped, whereas
\texttt{fit\$converged} records whether the numerical convergence criterion was
met. Termination by \texttt{max\_iter} is not convergence. If rollback is used,
\texttt{fit\$rolled\_back} records that the reported solution was restored to
the best objective iterate. Use the \texttt{seed} argument for deterministic
sequential runs.

\section{Visualization}

<<plots, echo=TRUE, fig=TRUE, height=4, width=6>>=
plot(fit, type = "objective")
plot(fit, type = "sizes")
@

\section{Current Limitations}

The package currently exports the audited sequential prototype implementation.
It does not run manuscript-scale simulations or real-data analyses in examples
or vignettes, and it does not export a parallel API until parallel RNG
reproducibility is separately validated.

\end{document}
